 {"id":"lem-limit-unique","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 3.11 states exactly that a convergent real sequence has a unique limit. The local proof adapts Hunter's epsilon/2 triangle-inequality contradiction to the library's rational-epsilon convention and ordered-field dependency interface.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
 {"id":"lem-convergent-implies-bounded","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"literature-derived","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 3.19 states exactly that every convergent real sequence is bounded, and its proof uses epsilon=1, the triangle inequality, and a maximum over the finitely many initial terms, the same argument used here with the harmless 0-based indexing adjustment.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
 {"id":"lem-convergent-implies-cauchy","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"literature-derived","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 3.46 states convergence if and only if Cauchyness and proves the forward direction by the same epsilon/2 triangle-inequality argument. The item isolates only that direction and uses rational epsilon, so its Statement is a faithful specialization rather than the source theorem verbatim.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
-{"id":"lem-null-times-bounded","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.ms.uky.edu/~lee/ma502/notes7/node3.html","https://people.reed.edu/~mayer/math112.html/html2/node12.html"],"rationale":"The Kentucky sequence-limit notes state exactly that the product of a bounded sequence and a null sequence is null; Reed's notes label the same result the null-times-bounded theorem. The local proof, including the M+1 device and real-to-rational epsilon bridge, is independently written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
-{"id":"thm-algebra-of-limits","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/5-1-sequences","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"OpenStax and the NKU notes give the standard sum, scalar, product, reciprocal, and quotient limit laws with the nonzero hypotheses. The item packages those laws together, adds difference explicitly, and states an eventual denominator lower bound; its proof adapts the standard null-sequence and reverse-triangle arguments to the library interface.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
-{"id":"lem-limit-preserves-order","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 3.22 and NKU Theorem 2-5 give preservation of non-strict inequalities by limits. The item allows the comparison only eventually and appends constant-sequence special cases. Its proof is an adapted one-sequence contradiction through y_k-x_k.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
-{"id":"thm-squeeze","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"literature-derived","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 3.23 and NKU Theorem 2-5(iv) state the squeeze theorem with pointwise bracketing. The item makes the standard finite-tail strengthening from pointwise to eventual bracketing. Its epsilon proof follows the sourced sandwich proof with a common threshold.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-null-times-bounded","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.reed.edu/~mayer/math112.html/html2/node12.html"],"rationale":"Reed College Math 112, Exercise 7.44, states exactly that the product of a bounded sequence and a null sequence is null. The local proof, including the M+1 device and real-to-rational epsilon bridge, is independently written. The previously recorded Kentucky URL returned HTTP 404 through both the orchestrator and model-side routes on 2026-08-04 and was omitted.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"thm-algebra-of-limits","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/5-1-sequences"],"rationale":"OpenStax gives the standard sum, scalar, product, reciprocal, and quotient limit laws with the nonzero hypotheses. The item packages those laws together, adds difference explicitly, and states an eventual denominator lower bound; its proof adapts the standard null-sequence and reverse-triangle arguments to the library interface. The stale NKU URL redirected to a canonical websites.nku.edu URL that the model-side A4 check could not fetch on 2026-08-04, so it was omitted.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
