+{"id":"lem-reciprocal-of-null-diverges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter gives the definitions and the standard positive null-sequence example 1/n alongside divergence to infinity. The item abstracts that standard reciprocal bridge into an iff for arbitrary positive sequences. The two-direction epsilon/M proof is locally composed. The stale NKU URL redirected to a canonical websites.nku.edu URL that the model-side A4 check could not fetch on 2026-08-04, so it was omitted.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-index-map-grows","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html","https://people.math.wisc.edu/~angenent/521.2016s/index.html"],"rationale":"The CMU notes define a subsequence by a strictly increasing index map, and the University of Wisconsin Math 521 notes explicitly state that a subsequence index sequence satisfies n_k>=k. The item also proves that consecutive strict comparisons imply global strict increase and shifts to 0-based indexing, so it is a semantic expansion of the standard index lemma. The previously recorded CUHK PDF returned HTTP 502 through the model-side A4 route on 2026-08-04 and was replaced.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-subsequence-inherits-limit","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 3.50 and CMU Proposition 4 state that every subsequence of a convergent sequence has the same limit; Hunter's following corollary supplies the distinct-subsequential-limits divergence test. The item combines both standard propositions and uses the 0-based n_k>=k formulation. The previously recorded CUHK PDF returned HTTP 502 through the model-side A4 route on 2026-08-04 and was omitted.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
 {"id":"rem-sequence-conventions","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limit_of_a_sequence","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"The sources collectively support the standard indexing, tail invariance, eventuality, uniqueness-before-limit-notation, bounded-range, divergence, rational/real epsilon, and subsequence conventions. The item is a library-specific synthesis with reading-order and dependency-routing commentary, not a novel mathematical claim.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
