+{"id":"fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter's order theorem preserves only non-strict inequalities and its standard 1/n example converges through positive terms to zero. The item packages these as the explicit false reversal and supplies an Archimedean epsilon proof, so it is a faithful standard counterexample with altered presentation. The stale NKU URL redirected to a canonical websites.nku.edu URL that the model-side A4 check could not fetch on 2026-08-04, so it was omitted.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"Hunter and CMU state the one-way inheritance theorem, and Hunter's alternating sequence has constant even and odd subsequences but does not converge. The item turns that standard witness into the explicit false converse and appends the corrected every-subsequence iff, so its Statement and proof are adapted rather than exact transcriptions. The previously recorded CUHK PDF returned HTTP 502 through the model-side A4 route on 2026-08-04 and was omitted.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}

